#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "25a73406-c869-5786-af97-cf90866eb1a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13101" :question/problem "Is it possible that the lengths of the sides of a triangle are $10, 15,$ and $25?$" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $10,15,25$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $25$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $10$ and $15.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n10 + 15 = 25 \\not> 25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $10,15,25$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f668f90-7140-5471-8638-18a38eae5e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Possible Lengths for a Side of a Triangle Given the Other Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "fa9395a9-f5b8-5c80-87e0-49da33706994" :question/math-academy-id "e-13102" :question/problem "A triangle has sides of length $1,7,$ and $x.$ Use the triangle inequality to determine all possible values of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Difference} & <x<\\text{Sum} \\\\ 7-1 & <x<7+1 \\\\ 6 & <x<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8e827c3-95a0-5270-8323-f2512b64edd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum or Minimum Length of a Side of a Triangle: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "dddb137b-afbb-515c-b44a-17b9ab2d667e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13103" :question/problem "Paul knows that the distance between cell tower $A$ and cell tower $B$ is $35\\,\\textrm{miles},$ and the distance between cell tower $B$ and cell tower $C$ is $25\\,\\textrm{miles},$ but he does not know their exact locations. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of possible values for the distance between cellphone towers $A$ and $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let: \n\n- $A$ be the position of the tower $A$\n\n- $B$ be the position of the tower $B$\n\n- $C$ be the position of the tower $C$\n\nLet's draw $\\triangle ABC.$\n\n![](images/a7/a74e768b739cf6ea497af934a44f0a5616da191b7f1a1c0979673a7ca891b0d6.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nAB = 35\\,\\textrm{mi},  \\qquad  BC = 25\\,\\textrm{mi}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-BC & <AC<AB+BC \\\\ 35-25 & <AC<35+25 \\\\ 10 & <AC<60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tower $A$ to the tower $C$ must be *between $10 \\,\\textrm{mi}$ and $60 \\,\\textrm{mi}$*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3736cf03-5a3a-5a22-8338-63cdabb1c046" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for a Length"}] :question/id #uuid "1512acb2-ac78-557a-b6a7-5b37addbeb24" :question/math-academy-id "e-13104" :question/problem "![](images/63/6301ab046f34d3261395d10ba73d31f62262c2362d0b088b38f67354568af97e.png)\n\nUsing the triangle inequality theorem only, what is the best upper bound that can be obtained for the length of $\\overline{AC}?$\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ADC}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & <AD+DC \\\\ AC & <26\\,\\text{cm}+13\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ AC & <39\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ABC}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & <AB+BC \\\\ AC & <12\\,\\text{cm}+10\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ AC & <22\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $AC <          39\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $AC <          22\\,\\textrm{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nAC <          22\\,\\textrm{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3736cf03-5a3a-5a22-8338-63cdabb1c046" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for a Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a47512d-a051-50d4-9fe9-2298713bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496675} {:answer/id #uuid "0db44782-2491-5de8-8e73-5c88b5612cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<8\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496684} {:answer/id #uuid "42afd28a-c2e0-583d-a833-67a0cfcc1014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496802} {:answer/id #uuid "b0edc692-4d2d-518a-ad04-d347f1b23c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497018} {:answer/id #uuid "ec90018f-6bf8-5b8c-864b-63a23e9d7fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec90018f-6bf8-5b8c-864b-63a23e9d7fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BD<9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497164} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7abfd0-0113-500f-bd9e-06cb4c3986b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80d38b14-c824-5c90-8288-ff609512d158" :question/math-academy-id "q-118632" :question/problem "![](images/99/99184bd48b6c36ddf3e2a818f428640deec3888879336a2207ced00b9830e808.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best upper bound for the length of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ obtained using the triangle inequality theorem only?\n\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BAD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & <BA+AD \\\\ BD & <4\\,\\text{cm}+5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BD & <9\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BCD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & <BC+CD \\\\ BD & <2\\,\\text{cm}+7\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BD & <9\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince in both cases $BD<9\\,\\text{cm}$  must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nBD<9\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8e827c3-95a0-5270-8323-f2512b64edd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum or Minimum Length of a Side of a Triangle: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157d2715-e648-570b-8361-6886ea52fe4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $10\\,\\text{m}$ and $39\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496704} {:answer/id #uuid "1e859382-40de-5d10-9e6d-c4966e11fa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $5\\,\\text{m}$ and $32\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496722} {:answer/id #uuid "80543135-fc7b-5662-b34f-91b074e2fcbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $9\\,\\text{m}$ and $41\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496922} {:answer/id #uuid "b46fb415-bc3f-5620-99fb-592dac4259ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $8\\,\\text{m}$ and $40\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497029} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bf1df9-b694-5129-aff1-cfbeffbd2338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $7\\,\\text{m}$ and $37\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9bf1df9-b694-5129-aff1-cfbeffbd2338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $7\\,\\text{m}$ and $37\\,\\text{m}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac6995c-1a6c-5ee1-9f33-24fa187cc427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bea2eff8-244e-52f3-ab2c-28db3e56b640" :question/math-academy-id "q-149914" :question/problem "In a gym, the tennis court is $15\\,\\text{m}$ away from the front desk, and the swimming pool is $22\\,\\text{m}$ away from the front desk. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of all possible values for the distance between the tennis court and the swimming pool?" :question/worked-solution "First, we let: \n\n- $F$ be the position of the front desk\n\n- $T$ be the position of the tennis court\n\n- $P$ be the position of the swimming pool\n\nLet's draw $△FTP.$\n\n![](images/a3/a388611f71e80942cf81f24a75a620ac6be8956aa5a69e8610bafe6b314712ca.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nFT=15\\,\\text{m},\\,FP=22\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FP-FT & <PT<FP+FT \\\\ 22-15 & <PT<22+15 \\\\ 7 & <PT<37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tennis court to the swimming pool must be *between $7\\,\\text{m}$ and $37\\,\\text{m}$*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f668f90-7140-5471-8638-18a38eae5e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Possible Lengths for a Side of a Triangle Given the Other Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0577b3bd-53e1-5518-ac1d-1c84e90a70da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496668} {:answer/id #uuid "5b27b5cd-ee7b-501e-874e-f9fff8aea7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496856} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfc3dff-d491-5615-b852-a7c0620ea77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496918} {:answer/id #uuid "b55d5871-ac21-5c8b-bbbe-6888210a0c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497030} {:answer/id #uuid "c172051f-1f8c-5c16-b919-626df0774ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b27b5cd-ee7b-501e-874e-f9fff8aea7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496856} :answer-field/id #uuid "5457db34-2430-56f0-a8b6-77158050958e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ad9a46e-5daf-5656-b0c7-6df1f4eb3fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-20607" :question/problem "Find the measure of the side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ of the triangle $△ABC$ if $AC=3.1$, $BC=0.7,$ and the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Difference} & <x<\\text{Sum} \\\\ 3.1-0.7 & <x<3.1+0.7 \\\\ 2.4 & <x<3.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only integer satisfying this condition is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f668f90-7140-5471-8638-18a38eae5e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Possible Lengths for a Side of a Triangle Given the Other Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d58248f-53fa-5abb-9c15-5e85db7bde87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $3$ and the largest is $13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496757} {:answer/id #uuid "3648c1cb-f3e2-5e94-b853-0381245e3e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $1$ and the largest is $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496775} {:answer/id #uuid "426724b2-997b-5c95-a10a-8b4cdc081e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $3$ and the largest is $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496801} {:answer/id #uuid "abcd35d5-56c0-569f-8a7b-13989edc2c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $3$ and the largest is $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497006} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6f3c8a-8530-5c5e-ba3d-ade43876430e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $2$ and the largest is $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abcd35d5-56c0-569f-8a7b-13989edc2c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The smallest is $3$ and the largest is $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497006} :answer-field/id #uuid "33150772-1cfd-5c03-afca-7b64aee3801a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7bebde0-d232-595f-80b4-6e89a72c1a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-52716" :question/problem "What are the smallest and the largest possible **integer** lengths of the third side of a triangle if the measures of the other two sides are $5$ and $7,$ respectively?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $x$ the unknown length of the third side.\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Difference} & <x<\\text{Sum} \\\\ 7-5 & <x<7+5 \\\\ 2 & <x<12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above inequalities are strict. So, $x$ can't be equal to $2$ or $12.$ Therefore, the smallest possible **integer** value is $3$ while the largest is $11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8e827c3-95a0-5270-8323-f2512b64edd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum or Minimum Length of a Side of a Triangle: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128bb07c-79bc-543b-ba53-8e454ab9fb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $8\\,\\text{km}$ and $24\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496698} {:answer/id #uuid "29f2858e-ee7c-53a3-9315-477ffd20bc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $6\\,\\text{km}$ and $18\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496753} {:answer/id #uuid "54d0983f-fb91-5a99-8a57-5995b1a24860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $6\\,\\text{km}$ and $24\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496838} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcefe64-4549-54bb-a483-e1a7003bec8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $6\\,\\text{km}$ and $20\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496868} {:answer/id #uuid "640e55ed-0859-5cb3-930a-6fab4abd5b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $8\\,\\text{km}$ and $20\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fcefe64-4549-54bb-a483-e1a7003bec8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "between $6\\,\\text{km}$ and $20\\,\\text{km}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496868} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb7fb09-2bad-52b4-9f93-a30d8456401c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cf326ee-935b-5f28-a3d8-7b44be0dc5b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-53103" :question/problem "Abe knows that he lives $13\\,\\text{km}$ away from Becky's house and $7\\,\\text{km}$ away from Chris' house, but doesn't know the distance between their houses. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of all possible values for the distance between Becky's house and Chris's house?" :question/worked-solution "First, we let: \n\n- $A$ be the position of Abe's house\n\n- $B$ be the position of Becky's house\n\n- $C$ be the position of Chris's house\n\nLet's draw $△ABC.$\n\n![](images/6b/6bd1335abf25d1d10da4e514c8c422aed987ad0bc6ce2afe3a7875ff7d5556c0.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nAB=13\\,\\text{km},\\,AC=7\\,\\text{km}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-AC & <BC<AB+AC \\\\ 13-7 & <BC<13+7 \\\\ 6 & <BC<20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between Becky's house and Chris's house, $BC,$ must be *between $6\\,\\text{km}$ and $20\\,\\text{km}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ce8c00-a678-5eb8-bc5d-b6e7a0bd06a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17,15,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496670} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8fa47c-ac7f-5809-b18c-322a9a1633e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17,9,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496867} {:answer/id #uuid "639053ea-eeb7-5da9-b9c2-9ce02e9e69e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,6,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496876} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd27dce-915b-5187-9d58-f17f1a0f46b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7,6,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496953} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a66b6f-4929-5c76-bd8f-df0a4783605a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11,8,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f8fa47c-ac7f-5809-b18c-322a9a1633e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17,9,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496867} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5df77ed-7aca-5bb7-9391-a75fd78a101f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19773a51-267a-5cef-93ba-8a45b8fbcd07" :question/math-academy-id "q-6086" :question/problem "Which three numbers **CANNOT** be the measures of the sides of a triangle?" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $17,9,8$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $17$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $9$ and $8.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n9+8=17≯17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $17,9,8$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c8b205-9919-551e-9499-711d42f12449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13,9,18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496694} {:answer/id #uuid "16a81ca6-4057-5b9d-99e1-9ee5f7e71bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7,11,12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496705} {:answer/id #uuid "298ff962-3ea7-5696-8453-8ece3f58c37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8,6,14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496750} {:answer/id #uuid "6c810880-cb1b-56e9-919a-77a2539f2dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,6,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496891} {:answer/id #uuid "759d1e49-242a-5385-98d7-0a9ddc8b09ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,10,11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "298ff962-3ea7-5696-8453-8ece3f58c37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8,6,14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496750} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96eb757-111f-5829-a83d-725d2f5b9e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa969a55-9c69-5f44-9f4d-b6d038b2d3b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76086" :question/problem "Which three numbers **CANNOT** be the measures of the sides of a triangle?" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $8,6,14$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $14$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $8$ and $6.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n8+6=14≯14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $8,6,14$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3736cf03-5a3a-5a22-8338-63cdabb1c046" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for a Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bd68e9-350e-5157-aad4-e3585ac0498b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496824} {:answer/id #uuid "6f01e06e-0414-50f2-800f-d3784949f655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<11\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496898} {:answer/id #uuid "c08858dd-92fa-534d-afa4-e3cb085ac47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<12\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497053} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d6c222-1d83-553e-b399-5fe123c8ff13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497070} {:answer/id #uuid "f59d4ae7-7608-53a0-b361-558fca4d06f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f01e06e-0414-50f2-800f-d3784949f655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC<11\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496898} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e08d31-403c-5b54-99d6-02ea85b0785d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "310044fc-a4be-5901-98cd-b60d9cb0dec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76098" :question/problem "![](images/06/06d5347f5827a4177b93fb633703ad0992cb85d938a9d88b1e2d55aa65b7beb3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best upper bound for the length of $\\overset{―}{BC},$ obtained using the triangle inequality theorem only?\n\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BAC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <BA+AC \\\\ BC & <8\\,\\text{cm}+3\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BC & <11\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BDC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <BD+DC \\\\ BC & <4\\,\\text{cm}+9\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BC & <13\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $BC<11\\,\\text{cm}$ and $BC<13\\,\\text{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nBC<11\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}]}
